summary:
TerraVision is a free geospatial decision-support platform for anyone who works with land in Uzbekistan: farmers, herders, land seekers and agronomists. A user draws a field on a map. TerraVision reads up to 20 years of its history from NASA and partner Earth observation data:

climate, frost, heat and drought from NASA POWER;
crop history from Sentinel-2 NDVI;
salinity, soil moisture (SMAP), water use (ECOSTRESS, MODIS) and terrain (SRTM);
snow-driven water supply signals (MODIS, GRACE);
ECMWF seasonal forecasts.
Its core answers the Field Shift challenge. It compares the current crop rotation with at least three alternatives on water, soil health, salinity, income and climate risk, and tests each one in dry and hot years. Every recommendation explains why it was suggested, the evidence, the benefits, the gaps and the risks.

Extra modules give herders pasture health and heat-stress alerts, let land seekers compare plots, and provide a work calendar with winter salt-leaching plans and a one-page "Land Passport".

This matters because Uzbekistan has about 1.75 million hectares of salinized land, shrinking water supply, and fields left bare after the wheat harvest. TerraVision turns satellite data into honest, local guidance.
